<ContentPage title="Techno Park" subtitle="Open-source R&D lab and maker space.">
  <p>
    The Techno Park is BARK's innovation engine — a space where appropriate technology is researched, prototyped,
    documented, and shared openly. Everything built here is open-source by default.
  </p>

  <h2>What We Build</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Appropriate technology</strong> — systems that can be maintained and replicated by a small community: solar arrays, water purification, small-scale manufacturing</li>
    <li><strong>Open hardware</strong> — 3D printers, CNC machines, electronics labs for prototyping and local production</li>
    <li><strong>Software infrastructure</strong> — communication systems, data management, community tools — all self-hosted and open-source</li>
    <li><strong>Agricultural tech</strong> — greenhouse automation, soil monitoring, aquaponics, seed banking</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How It Works</h2>
  <p>
    Resident makers and visiting researchers collaborate in shared workshop spaces. Every project is documented
    as open-source — plans, materials lists, build guides, test results. If BARK discovers a better way to purify
    water or generate power, that knowledge belongs to everyone.
  </p>

  <h2>DIY Ethos</h2>
  <p>
    We don't buy what we can build. We don't hoard what we can share. The Techno Park embodies the principle
    that technology sovereignty — the ability to understand, maintain, and improve your own systems — is a
    prerequisite for genuine autonomy.
  </p>

  <div class="cta-box">
    <p class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 mb-2">Makers, engineers, researchers</p>
    <p>If you build things and want them to matter, <a href="/en/participate">we want to hear from you</a>.</p>
  </div>
</ContentPage>
